{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact head 11b2fbb3666a0d8a7c8e75c8481b4c221918b8b8 for notes/2026-08-30-outcome-map.md. The note is sound in its bones: it refuses to parse conditions prose, refuses to synthesize a blocked state, keeps waits-on to what the fold names, keeps population controls and search outside the graph, and states ten testable invariants. I checked its relation claims against what the projection actually serves and against the documented layer contract, and four things must change before this note can be the basis for implementation requests. ONE, ARCHITECTURE: the note says it changes no authority boundary, which is true of layers one to five, but it changes the documented layer-seven contract. docs/reference/architecture.md line 1467 states the UI layer as exactly two screens, a sortable list and one thread spine, and notes/2026-08-19-ui-simplification.md records no presentation switch as an acceptance criterion (lines 86 and 378, landed at 3a45e1f2). A table-or-graph choice and a third presentation reverse both. The note must say so explicitly, argue the reversal on its merits against the simplification note's reasoning that a second layout of the same rows is configuration rather than information, and require that the first implementing head update architecture.md line 1467 in the same head with its candidate artifact, per AGENTS.md step four. TWO, RATIFICATION GATE: the definition admits a dependent resting on a ratification act, or on a reviewed chain whose cross-thread gate is that act. The second clause is a chain walk, which contradicts the note's own rule that no transitive edge is drawn and would let the browser name a relation the fold does not project (architecture.md lines 1359-1361). The fold already projects the fact needed: every statement carries ratified and ratified_by, the act now in force (internal/workroom/fold.go lines 80-110, whose comment forbids rebuilding which ratify counts from acts). Define the gate as a direct basis that is either a ratify act or a statement whose projected ratified_by is set, and drop the chain clause. THREE, SUPERSESSION: the projection types only the act's target; the replacement role is not typed, as ui/src/lib/supersedeLinks.ts lines 16-31 already records, and the only typed successor is commitments.successor_request, which exists for linked refiles alone and has one live instance. The dashed line may therefore say what an act retired (act to target); it may not say what replaced it unless that is the act's own recorded basis, drawn as a direct-basis edge, or successor_request. Say that, and forbid the sole-other-basis heuristic from being rendered as a fact. FOUR, VOCABULARY AND ROOTS: provenance is an untyped list of bases (Projection.Provenance, fold.go line 268). The tooltip wording, A is a direct basis for B, recorded as B rests on A, is exactly right; the section headings and prose that call this the task-flow edge, child request, or work that flowed from a parent are not, and if any of those words reach the screen the map names a dependency the fold never projected. Make the fold's words, rests on, ratified by, superseded, the only rendered relation vocabulary and keep child, depends on and flows from out of the normative text. Relatedly, the root rule, no incoming displayed relation, makes a search-filtered child look top-level, which is the misleading case the note's own review question asks about; require the card to distinguish root of this view from no recorded basis, the basis chip alone does not. SECURITY: read-only presentation over the projection the browser already receives; no new endpoint, write path, credential or authority; the layer-seven rule that the browser derives no authority of its own is preserved once point four holds. Two bounds to state: /v0/status is about 30.7 MB at depth 14695 with 14695 provenance keys, so build the collapsed graph once per frontier and scope it to the selected population plus direct context, never the whole log; and waiting_on is a fingerprint set on 4 of the 20 live rows and deliberately empty for awaiting-merge and open (fold.go lines 3202-3210), so most cards will honestly read not named — the note should say that up front so nobody reads sparsity as a bug. SIMPLIFICATION: the slice order is right and slice one, the graph projection contract with deterministic fixtures and no drawing, is the whole value; require it to reuse ui/src/lib/threads.ts's thread membership rule (first-basis walk cut at commitment crossings) rather than define a second notion of thread, and consider whether the selection rail in slice three earns its place before the thread view has been tried as the only detail surface. The premise of thirty-plus open requests is historical, the live board is 20 rows today with 110 recorded earlier, which supports the fixture-first plan. Fix the four points in a successor head and I will re-review promptly.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6745baa3394912058d67c90a6204c4de8510310e","head":"11b2fbb3666a0d8a7c8e75c8481b4c221918b8b8","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ef3910a41458761af2eeebf3e6967c2045d1897c\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ef3910a41458761af2eeebf3e6967c2045d1897c","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}